.TH KAKIKOMI 1 "2026-10-27" "kakikomi" "User Commands"
.SH NAME
kakikomi \- write disk images to removable media, verified and never mounted
.SH SYNOPSIS
.B kakikomi
.br
.B kakikomi
.RI [ options ] " image" | url | "device " [ target ...]
.br
.B kakikomi format
.RI [ options ] " target " [ filesystem ] " " [ label ]
.br
.B kakikomi release
.br
.B kakikomi doctor
.br
.B kakikomi version
.br
.B kakikomi help
.SH DESCRIPTION
.B kakikomi
writes a disk image, a download or another drive to one or more removable
disks. Every chunk is written, read back and compared before the next, and
the whole disk is compared with the image's SHA-256 at the end. The target is
never mounted, not even for a moment.
.PP
Compressed images in gzip, xz, zstd, bzip2 and zip form are read as they are
written. Virtual disks in VHD, VHDX, VMDK and QCOW2 form are converted first.
A Windows installer ISO is laid out as a UEFI-bootable FAT32 drive unless
.B \-\-raw
is given.
.PP
A disk is named by its device, such as
.IR /dev/disk4 ,
or by any volume mounted from it, such as
.IR /Volumes/USB ,
which stands for the whole disk.
.PP
Before anything is written, kakikomi prints what it is about to replace and
asks for the device names to be typed back.
.SH COMMANDS
.TP
.B format
Quick format a disk: write only the new filesystem's structures. Without a
filesystem, disks up to 32 GiB get FAT32 and larger ones exFAT.
.B floppy
writes a 1.44 MB filesystem with no partition table.
.TP
.B release
Remove the mount guards an interrupted run left behind.
.TP
.B doctor
List the tools kakikomi needs and whether each is installed. Exits non-zero
only when a required tool is missing.
.TP
.B version
Print the version and the platform.
.TP
.B help
Print the usage page.
.SH OPTIONS
.TP
.BR \-\-verify ", " \-\-no\-verify
Read the whole disk back and compare its SHA-256 at the end. Default yes.
.TP
.BR \-\-delete ", " \-\-no\-delete
Move the image to the Trash once every disk validated. Default no.
.TP
.B \-\-again
Write the same image to the next disk inserted.
.TP
.BR \-\-reinsert ", " \-\-no\-reinsert
After ejecting, wait for the disk to come back and compare it again.
Default no.
.TP
.B \-\-zero\-tail
Blank the space past the image on a larger disk.
.TP
.BI \-\-sha256 " hash"
Stop unless the image matches this SHA-256. Without it, a checksum file
published next to the image is read; see
.BR "PUBLISHED CHECKSUMS" .
.TP
.B \-\-allow\-internal
Allow an internal disk. The disk holding the running system is always refused.
.TP
.B \-\-keep\-guard
Keep macOS from ever mounting the disk again.
.TP
.B \-\-raw
Write a Windows ISO as is instead of as a FAT32 installer.
.PP
Every option that takes a value also takes the form
.IR \-\-name = value ,
and
.B \-\-
ends the options.
.SH PUBLISHED CHECKSUMS
Before anything is written, kakikomi looks next to the image for a checksum
file that names it: a file named after the image, such as
.I debian.iso.sha256
or
.IR debian.sha512 ,
or a list such as
.IR SHA256SUMS ,
.IR MD5SUMS ,
.IR b2sums.txt ,
a Fedora
.I CHECKSUM
or an
.I .sfv
file. For an image given as a URL, the same names are asked of its server.
.PP
The lines written by
.BR sha256sum ,
.BR md5sum ,
.BR b2sum ,
.BR shasum ,
BSD tag lines, OpenSSL,
.B CertUtil
and PowerShell are read, along with PGP-signed lists, Gentoo
.I DIGESTS
files, SFV files and files holding only a digest. MD5, SHA-1, SHA-224,
SHA-256, SHA-384, SHA-512, SHA3, BLAKE2b and CRC32 are recognized. SHA-256 is
checked first when published, otherwise the strongest algorithm a tool here
can compute. A compressed image matches either its file or its contents.
.PP
A mismatch stops the run before any disk is touched, and so does a published
algorithm no installed tool computes. A digest in a file that names no image
only warns, since it may belong to another download. The PGP signature of a
signed list is not verified.
.SH ENVIRONMENT
.BR KAKIKOMI_VERIFY ,
.BR KAKIKOMI_DELETE ,
.BR KAKIKOMI_AGAIN ,
.BR KAKIKOMI_REINSERT ,
.BR KAKIKOMI_ZERO_TAIL ,
.BR KAKIKOMI_SHA256 ,
.BR KAKIKOMI_ALLOW_INTERNAL ,
.B KAKIKOMI_KEEP_GUARD
and
.B KAKIKOMI_MODE=raw
set the same options, with
.B y
or
.B n
for yes or no. An option on the command line wins over the environment.
.SH EXIT STATUS
0 when every disk was written and verified, 1 on any failure or refusal.
.SH SEE ALSO
.BR kiyome (1),
.BR dd (1)
